WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:07.700
Hello! If you are here to join our little&nbsp;
lesson about the recent advances in the&nbsp;

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.480
Natural Language Processing (or short NLP) field,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.630
you are exactly at the right place!

00:00:14.630 --> 00:00:19.010
We will follow the young, but explosive life of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:23.540
the Transformer architecture that&nbsp;
revolutionized the AI field of NLP!

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:30.740
It all started a few years ago, where the fun&nbsp;
in AI was happening in Computer Vision with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:37.580
great success of CNNs on the ImageNet challenge&nbsp;
(I am talking about you, AlexNet in 2013!), the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:44.660
success of Neural Style Transfer and of Generative&nbsp;
Adversarial Networks, and the list can go on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:50.390
on! Computer Vision was attracting headlines and&nbsp;
NLP was not so much in the public’s attention.&nbsp;

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:54.710
Of course, NLP researchers
were working hard too.&nbsp;

00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:02.390
For example, 2013 was the year ow Word2Vec,
based on the idea that neural networks should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:08.060
be forced to learn similarities between&nbsp;
words based on word distributions.&nbsp;

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:16.610
For example, the word “Apple” is often used&nbsp;
near the same words as Microsoft or IBM, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:23.450
it is mapped close to these in the vector space.
But “Apple” is also used often with words like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:30.560
“pear” or other fruits, so we see that we can not&nbsp;
disambiguate words contextually with this method.&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.620
2014 and 2015 were marked by the&nbsp;
rising popularity of the Recurrent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.110
Neural Network (RNN), that made it feasible&nbsp;
to not only solve classification tasks better,&nbsp;

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.410
but also tasks for sequence to sequence modelling.&nbsp;

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:50.750
An example for generating a sequence&nbsp;
from a sequence is Machine Translation.&nbsp;

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:56.690
The idea of Recurrent Neural Network&nbsp;
or RNNs is simply put the following:&nbsp;

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:01.640
The same Neural Network block is applied&nbsp;
recurrently on the input sequence.&nbsp;

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:08.720
Reaching the sequence end, the module ideally&nbsp;
has learnt one single vector that captures the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:12.500
meaning of the whole sentence.
And from this single vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.940
one can start to translate, by&nbsp;
reverting the process in the decoder.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:22.250
But one problem of the method is:
It works well for short sentences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:27.200
like “I am working hard” that can&nbsp;
perhaps be captured by a single vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.350
but not for sentences like the second one.
The RNN block, reaching the end of the sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:37.910
is so “overwhelmed” by the new input, that it&nbsp;
forgets what the beginning of the sentence was.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:43.220
2015-2016 were the years that&nbsp;
took care of this “forgetting”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.100
problem by employing the attention mechanism.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.550
This attention is nothing more than a way to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.970
which part of the sentence&nbsp;
should get more importance.&nbsp;

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:58.910
In other words, attention tells the RNN&nbsp;
what it should focus on and not forget.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:04.700
Yet another problem with Recurrent Neural&nbsp;
Networks is that they are… recurrent!

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.510
Unfortunately, sentences&nbsp;
are processed sequentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.780
word by word, like generally humans do.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:15.380
For a machine, this means&nbsp;
however, that it needs a lot of&nbsp;

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.930
time to process huge corpora of text.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:23.510
And here it is where 2017, bam!&nbsp;
The finally long-awaited ImageNet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.170
moment came also for NLP with&nbsp;
the Transformer architecture!

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.370
It first crashed the party with
the “Attention is all you need” paper!

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:38.900
Like the title says: If RNNs and attention are so&nbsp;
slow because of the sequential processing of RNNs,&nbsp;

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.760
let us just use only the attention&nbsp;
and throw away the RNN part!

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.760
And Boom, here it was, the Transformer!

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.720
In practice this is a little&nbsp;
more complicated than this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.050
we will explain the Transformer&nbsp;
architecture in the next video.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:04:00.710
Here, we put it very simply: Transformers,&nbsp;
like RNNs handle sequence data, but not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:06.560
in a particular order. This means, that it&nbsp;
can train much faster with parallelization!

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.830
Now, focusing less on how it works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:14.690
we focus what the Transformer means and&nbsp;
what came out of the original transformer:

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:18.500
Because this architecture and it’s&nbsp;
descendants prove to be improving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.330
the state-of-the-art across the board, like in&nbsp;
machine translation, sentiment classification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:27.860
coreference resolution, commonsense&nbsp;
reasoning, and so on and so on!

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:32.220
It has been even successfully&nbsp;
applied on translating from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.180
one programming language to another
or for solving symbolic mathematics!&nbsp;

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.600
And if you want to see the transformer in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.320
do not hesitate to play around&nbsp;
with demos, like this one here.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:50.610
Coming back to our history lesson: 2018 Google&nbsp;
Researchers developed a bidirectional version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:56.340
of the Transformer and they called it "BERT".&nbsp;
Yes, you heard right, BERT from Sesame Street.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:00.870
Now let’s not get distracted by the&nbsp;
weird names researchers choose for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:07.179
their model and let us focus on the word&nbsp;
“bidirectional” regarding the transformer.

00:05:07.179 --> 00:05:12.150
Bidirectional means, that it allows&nbsp;
the information to flow forwards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:17.010
backwards as the model trains, which&nbsp;
results in better model performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.810
Versions of BERT are one of the&nbsp;
most successful advances in NLP.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:29.850
Like Word2Vec, BERT is also mapping similar&nbsp;
words closely, but it is context sensitive! This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:35.700
means that a different word vector is computed&nbsp;
for a word if it is encountered in different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:44.100
contexts. For example, if we have two sentences
like “This game is just not fair” and “I had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.830
great time at this fair”, the word “fair” will&nbsp;
have very different word vectors with BERT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:54.660
but equal-valued word vectors&nbsp;
would be computed by Word2Vec.&nbsp;

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.370
And after BERT, everything just escalated&nbsp;
for attention-based architectures:&nbsp;

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:07.710
In 2019, researchers developed RoBERTa, ERNIE&nbsp;
2.0, XLNet, and this to only to name a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:13.530
small fraction! All improve upon BERT&nbsp;
both conceptionally and in performance.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.330
But these architectures just keep coming,&nbsp;
there is no light at the end of the tunnel yet.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:23.460
And now to make it even crazier, we&nbsp;
close the circle by going back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:28.110
computer vision community, because the&nbsp;
Transformer is also making waves there:&nbsp;

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.670
The Transformer, proved to be&nbsp;
so performant on text sequences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:38.610
that it was also applied to pixel sequences,&nbsp;
or as we usually called them, images!

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:44.420
And wait, there is even more: If we can&nbsp;
apply transformers on text or images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:50.600
why not apply them on text and images&nbsp;
simultaneously??? Here we are in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:57.170
so-called realm of multi-modality, where the goal&nbsp;
is to process different input sources at once.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:08.180
So still 2019 was the year of V-LBert,&nbsp;
VisualBERT, ViLBERT, UNITER and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:14.030
just to name a few! All these papers came&nbsp;
out around the same month of August 2019.&nbsp;

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:19.400
Three of them on the same day!
What a time to be an NLP researcher!&nbsp;

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:24.020
Hard to be not both overwhelmed and&nbsp;
overly excited at the same time!

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:30.860
To finally end this crazy enumeration: The&nbsp;
transformer works so well on multiple tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.990
and in many cases even though it was not&nbsp;
explicitly trained on the task of interest.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:42.350
So, we are now at the point in NLP where we have&nbsp;
to investigate if a neural network trained on task&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:50.090
A is also able to more or less solve a task B.
For more information on how to “probe” for this,&nbsp;

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:54.650
check out our previous video, linked here&nbsp;
in the video and in the description below!

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.820
If you want to want to know more about&nbsp;
the inner workings of the Transformer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:00.620
wait until our next video!&nbsp;

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:05.120
Thanks for watching, do not forget to&nbsp;
like and subscribe and see you next time!

